TI  - Metformin, the Aspirin of the 21st Century: Its Role in Gestational Diabetes Mellitus, Prevention of Preeclampsia and Cancer, and the Promotion of Longevity
T2  - OBSTETRICAL & GYNECOLOGICAL SURVEY
AB  - Metformin (dimethylbiguanide hydrochloride) was initially introduced as an antidiabetic agent; however, in recent years, it has shown a wide array of beneficial effects ranging from cancer therapy to extending longevity. As a gestational antidiabetic agent, metformin has compared favorably to insulin by showing a significantly reduced frequency of neonatal hyperglycemia, large-for-gestational-age neonates, and admissions to a neonatal intensive care unit compared with those treated with insulin. Metformin is also known to have therapeutic properties against polycystic ovary syndrome, congestive heart failure, chronic liver and kidney disease, multiple sclerosis, renal tubulointerstitial fibrosis, and nonalcoholic fatty liver disease. Recent studies also reveal metformin to be effective in treating and preventing preeclampsia.
   This article discusses the history of metformin and analyzes its mechanism of action in a variety of processes. The literature reviewed shows 2 primary mechanisms: suppression of intracellular metabolic activity of mitochondria and the cellular nutrient-sensing system mediated by mechanistic target of rapamycin (mTOR). This article reviews the results of metaanalyses on the role of metformin in preventing gestational hypertension and gestational diabetes mellitus (GDM), as well as in treating obese, nondiabetic pregnant women to prevent preeclampsia. One randomized controlled clinical trial showed that although metformin did not reduce the frequency of large-for-gestational-age neonates, it significantly reduced the frequency of preeclampsia and also contributed toward reduction in maternal weight gain. One study cited showed that metformin reduced the production of sFlt-1 and soluble endoglin by endothelial cells, villous trophoblast, and villous explants. The administration of sFlt-1 or soluble endoglin to animals produces changes characteristic of preeclampsia, and this report suggested that metformin regulates these antiangiogenic factors at the mitochondrial level.
   Metformin could also prevent preeclampsia by modifying the cellular nutrient-sensing system utilized by the placenta. This system is mediated by mTOR and involves the mechanistic target rapamycin, an mTOR inhibitor. The activation of mTOR is positively correlated with first-trimester maternal body mass index, which links maternal overnutrition and placental nutrient sensing. In addition, down-regulation of mTOR in the placenta has been seen in small-for-gestational-age fetuses. It is unclear whether this is the effect of a primary defect in the sensing mechanism or an adaptive response. Metaanalyses studying the teratogenic effect of metformin on embryonic growth showed that metformin did not increase the risk of congenital malformations and fetal or neonatal complications, indicating the safety of metformin use during pregnancy. Although studies have shown that metformin targets several age-related pathways, the mechanism by which it prolongs life are still unclear. Although showing promising results in many arenas, further research is required to elucidate the mechanism of action of metformin.
