TI  - Maternal Diabetes During Pregnancy and Early Onset of Cardiovascular Disease in Offspring: Population Based Cohort Study With 40 Years of Follow Up
T2  - OBSTETRICAL & GYNECOLOGICAL SURVEY
AB  - The primary cause of worldwide mortality and morbidity is cardiovascular disease (CVD). Recently, increases in CVD have disproportionately affected children and young adults, who have experienced the most drastic upticks in disease incidence. There is growing evidence that maternal health before and during pregnancy greatly influences offspring CVD risk. Specifically, pregestational and gestational diabetes mellitus (GDM) in mothers can translate to their offspring as diseases such as congenital heart disease, obesity, and diabetes, all of which are risk factors for CVD. This Danish study aimed to determine the relationship between maternal diabetes and CVD risk in offspring-specifically whether or not maternal diabetes is associated with an increased risk.
   Danish national health registries were utilized for conduction of a population-based cohort study. The registry data allowed for analysis of Denmark's 2,432,000 live births between the years 1977 and 2016. Offspring were followed by the study until they met any of the following outcomes: CVD diagnosis, emigration, death, or the study end date of 40 years in December 2016. Maternal diabetes was defined as an umbrella term for both gestational and pregestational diabetes, and the latter included type 1 or type 2 diabetes prior to gestation. Further, participants with pregestational diabetes were divided into 2 groups: 1 or multiple complications. Offspring with congenital heart disease were excluded from the study.
   One thousand one hunderd fifty-three offspring of diabetic mothers and 91,211 offspring of healthy mothers experienced CVD diagnoses throughout the study's 40 years of surveillance. Offspring unexposed to maternal diabetes at 40 years old experienced a cumulative incidence of 13.1%. The group delivered to women with diabetes experienced a cumulative incidence of 17.8%; there was a 4.7% incidence difference between the exposed and unexposed offspring groups. Controlling for potential confounding factors, there was a hazard ratio of 1.29 for developing early-onset CVD (95% confidence interval, 1.21-1.37) between offspring of women with and without diabetes in pregnancy. Through sibship analysis, the study team found an association between both pregestational and GDM with increased CVD diagnostic rates in offspring. Additionally, early-onset CVD conditions such as hypertensive disease (1.78 [95% confidence interval, 1.50-2.11]), heart failure (1.45 [0.89-2.35]), deep vein thrombosis (1.82 [1.38-2.41]), and pulmonary embolism (1.91 [1.31-2.80]) were all increased. Further, the rates of these outcomes proved more extreme among offspring with mothers who experienced diabetic complications (1.60 [1.25-2.05]). Finally, early-onset CVD in offspring was greater in diabetic mothers with comorbid CVD (1.73 [1.36-2.20]). This finding is associated with the impact of comorbid CVD, but the relationship between diabetes and CVD was not significant (P value for interaction = 0.94).
   The association between maternal diabetes and rates of early-onset CVD in offspring can be utilized to guide clinical care and potentially impact outcomes. Specifically, improvements in the prevention and treatment of diabetes for women during their reproductive years may lead to improvements in the rates of CVD of their offspring.
