TI  - Hyperhomocysteinemia in diabetic pregnancy
T2  - GIORNALE ITALIANO DI OSTETRICIA E GINECOLOGIA
AB  - Homocysteine is an amino acid that is involved in several key metabolic processes. Blood concentrations of homocysteine are determined by various dietary factors, including folic acid and vitamin B12; alterations in physiology, such as renal impairment; and variation in the activity of enzymes in the various pathways as a result of genetic polymorphisms. In normal pregnancy, homocysteine concentrations decrease. Disturbance of maternal and fetal homocysteina metabolism is associated with fetal neural tube defects, with various conditions characterized by placental vasculopaty, such as pre-eclampsia and abruption; and with recurrent pregnancy loss. The combination of hyperhomocysteinemia and abnormal glucose tolerance may increase the risk of placental vasculopathy in pre-eclampsia. Therefore, the goal of this study was to evaluate the possible association between plasma-total homocysteine and gestational diabetes or glucose intolerance in normotensive and preeclamptic pregnant women. A prospective study included 50 pregnant women who came to the Department of Mycrobiologic Science and Gynaecologic Science of the University of Catania during the period covering from January 2004 to March 2005. The O' Sullivan test and oral glucose tolerance test was performed; and homocysteine, folate and vitamin B12 were measured from the 20th to the 34th weeks of pregnancy. Significant differences were observed in women with gestational diabetes complicated by hypertension (GD + PIH), while in normotensive patients the glucose intolerance group did showed significant differences.
