TI  - Ultrasound Evaluation of Pregnancies with Single Umbilical Artery (SUA)
T2  - 5TH ROMANIAN CONGRESS OF THE ROMANIAN SOCIETY OF ULTRASOUND IN OBSTETRICS AND GYNECOLOGY
CP  - 5th Romanian Congress of the Romanian-Society-of-Ultrasound-in-Obstetrics-and-Gynecology
AB  - The single umbilical artery (SUA) can be diagnosed before birth by ultrasound in up to 2% of all pregnancies and, when associated with other foetal structural defects, there is an increased risk of chromosomal abnormalities and adverse pregnancy outcome. The finding of a SUA should alert the obstetrician to search for associated malformations and markers of chromosomal defects.
   The frequency of chromosomal abnormalities is extremely rare in isolated SUA cases. The risk for congenital heart disease after the detection of SUA in an otherwise apparently normal foetus from an unselected population seems small and may not warrant specialist foetal echocardiography.
   Isolated SUA (ISUA) appears to be an independent predictor of adverse perinatal outcomes such as small for gestational age, oligohydramnios, polyhydramnios, gestational diabetes mellitus and perinatal mortality. An ISUA is associated with an adverse pregnancy outcome, but this however does not decrease the rate of vaginal delivery in full-term births.
   Careful ultrasound examination is crucial to detect adverse perinatal outcomes in patients with SUA. Foetal karyotyping must be performed by amniocentesis in cases with additional anomalies, soft marker or positive prenatal screening test. However foetal karyotyping is not recommended for cases with isolated SUA.
